00:00:28.098 --> 00:00:30.150
Hello, and welcome.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:36.766
You may have noticed that I am not one of&nbsp;
your regular hosts, and you are correct.

00:00:36.766 --> 00:00:42.704
My name is Sarah Suta and you can&nbsp;
normally see me over at Bizarre Beasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.704 --> 00:00:46.800
but I heard that James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.969
had found something a little more macro&nbsp;
than usual and I wanted to take a look.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:58.620
I actually spent a good number of years looking&nbsp;
through dissecting microscopes at everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:06.891
from beetles to mosquitos, and I'm excited to see&nbsp;
what he found at an even greater magnification.

00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:10.320
Whenever we venture together&nbsp;
into the microscopic world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:18.090
as we often do on this channel, we never fail to&nbsp;
find both beauty and mystery in equal measure.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:24.383
It's pretty much the only guarantee we have&nbsp;
when we point our microscope at a new sample.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:30.974
We don't know exactly what we'll see, but we know&nbsp;
it will leave us enchanted and full of questions.

00:01:30.974 --> 00:01:34.440
And the particular beautiful&nbsp;
mystery that we’re exploring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.127
in this episode came to us via an unlikely source:

00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:41.924
a gang of ruthless cats…

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:48.118
Let me explain.

00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:53.331
See, a little while ago, James found&nbsp;
himself with a bit of a problem.

00:01:53.331 --> 00:01:57.780
He was keeping some wheat grains at&nbsp;
home to use as food for the microbes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.762
that he cultures and films for our enjoyment.

00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:07.249
But before he could feed the grains to his&nbsp;
microbes, they became infested with the larvae..

00:02:07.249 --> 00:02:09.000
of moths.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.630
And while James worked to find the infestation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.820
his pet cats took it upon themselves to&nbsp;
deal with the situation in their own way.

00:02:19.313 --> 00:02:24.159
What you’re looking at now, bursting&nbsp;
with color like the northern lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:29.573
are the wing fragments of some moths&nbsp;
that were slain by James’s cats.

00:02:29.573 --> 00:02:34.140
Because what does a microscopist&nbsp;
do when they find themselves with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.145
an unexpected abundance of moth wings?

00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:42.540
Well, you guessed it, they place&nbsp;
the fragments under a microscope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.942
and attempt to reveal their secrets, of course.

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:52.140
But what could be that complex or enigmatic&nbsp;
about something as everyday as a moth wing?

00:02:53.693 --> 00:03:00.792
It turns out, the wings of moths contain many&nbsp;
secrets that science has yet to fully explain.

00:03:00.792 --> 00:03:08.054
Like, for example, the reason behind this&nbsp;
mesmerizing fluorescence unfolding in front of us.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.340
After all, you would be forgiven for generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.847
thinking of moths as rather&nbsp;
drab, as far as insects go.

00:03:15.847 --> 00:03:20.564
They’re not the most vibrant or&nbsp;
dazzling of critters to our eyes.

00:03:20.564 --> 00:03:23.640
But it turns out that perception is more a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.994
of the limitations of our sight&nbsp;
than of their actual appearance.

00:03:28.994 --> 00:03:35.451
Because, under ultraviolet light, the&nbsp;
wings of these moths glow like an aurora.

00:03:35.451 --> 00:03:40.634
Both how and why this happens,&nbsp;
no one is honestly entirely sure.

00:03:40.634 --> 00:03:44.707
Perhaps by exciting the wings&nbsp;
with high energy UV light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.707 --> 00:03:49.195
their scales release fluorescent&nbsp;
particles into the water of the sample.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:55.560
These particles absorb UV and re-emit the&nbsp;
energy as visible light in the form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:02.880
vivid greens and oranges, allowing us to glimpse&nbsp;
the hidden beauty and vibrant colouration that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.814
under normal circumstances,&nbsp;
would be totally invisible to us.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:13.380
And, because the wing scales themselves&nbsp;
are hydrophobic by nature, the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:20.211
bursts with mists of fluorescent particles,&nbsp;
resulting in the waves of light we see here.

00:04:20.211 --> 00:04:25.394
Another idea is that the wing scales&nbsp;
have some sort of innate UV reflection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.394 --> 00:04:30.701
or absorption properties, and what we’re&nbsp;
seeing are cones of light reflected by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:35.599
deforming nanostructures that are&nbsp;
responsible for those properties.

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:40.260
See, it might be surprising to learn&nbsp;
that the overlapping microscopic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.817
wing scales of moths and butterflies are&nbsp;
actually one of their defining features.

00:04:45.817 --> 00:04:51.383
Their scientific name - lepidoptera - is&nbsp;
a reflection of this characteristic trait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.383 --> 00:04:54.916
it literally translates to ‘scaly wings’.

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:59.460
You might have even noticed this before,&nbsp;
if you’ve ever held a butterfly or moth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:03.182
they might have left behind&nbsp;
a fine powder on your hand.

00:05:03.182 --> 00:05:08.377
That powder is made up of hundreds&nbsp;
or thousands of these wing scales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.377 --> 00:05:12.000
which we’re looking at right&nbsp;
now at high magnification.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.487
And the intricate, complex&nbsp;
nanostructures that line the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.487 --> 00:05:20.430
scales of their wings have been found&nbsp;
to have some interesting advantages.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.696
For example, they play very strange&nbsp;
roles in their struggle for survival.

00:05:25.696 --> 00:05:31.463
Some species possess elaborate nanostructures&nbsp;
on their scales that interact with sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:37.489
rather than light, providing them with a&nbsp;
form of sophisticated acoustic camouflage.

00:05:37.489 --> 00:05:43.140
For tens of millions of years, they’ve&nbsp;
been targeted by echolocating bats that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.760
zero in on prey by bouncing&nbsp;
soundwaves off their bodies.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.220
So, in response, the wing scales of&nbsp;
some moth species have evolved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:59.199
absorb these soundwaves, cloaking their&nbsp;
acoustic signature from unfriendly ears.

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:05.640
Perhaps the wing structures we’re looking at now,&nbsp;
which interact with light waves, also evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:12.508
for a similar defensive role in this species,&nbsp;
scattering light as a means of visual camouflage

00:06:12.508 --> 00:06:19.064
Or dazzling and obscuring the sight of potential&nbsp;
predators that can perceive certain wavelengths.

00:06:19.064 --> 00:06:25.131
Though, unfortunately for these particular&nbsp;
moths, it clearly doesn't work against cats…

00:06:25.131 --> 00:06:29.848
But predator avoidance is just&nbsp;
one of many possibilities.

00:06:29.848 --> 00:06:35.220
Lepidoptera are an ancient and diverse&nbsp;
bunch, and their scaly wings are adapted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.847
for a range of functions that we’re&nbsp;
only just beginning to understand.

00:06:39.847 --> 00:06:45.090
Some seem to use their UV reflecting&nbsp;
wings not to hide or confuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.575
but the total opposite - to communicate.

00:06:48.575 --> 00:06:54.000
Like many other insect groups - but&nbsp;
unlike us - the eyes of lepidopterans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.370
are exquisitely sensitive to the UV&nbsp;
part of the electromagnetic spectrum.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.780
It’s the reason why researchers and&nbsp;
hobbyists will often collect them by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:10.706
shining a UV blacklight against a sheet of fabric,&nbsp;
drawing in a range of species from far and wide.

00:07:10.706 --> 00:07:15.840
And various lepidopterans are thought to&nbsp;
use their UV perception and manipulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:21.704
abilities to signal amongst each other&nbsp;
for mate attraction, species recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.704 --> 00:07:25.740
and other secret messages we&nbsp;
can only guess the meaning of.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.866
So maybe the aurora we’re gazing at is more like&nbsp;
fragments of a language that we can’t decipher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.866 --> 00:07:37.304
brimming with information that&nbsp;
only other moths can comprehend.

00:07:37.304 --> 00:07:40.849
But the truth is, we simply don’t know.

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:43.440
For now at least, the beautiful mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.298
of this moth wing light-show&nbsp;
reveals just one clear message:

00:07:48.298 --> 00:07:51.252
we still have so much left to learn.

